Now I understand the vulnerability. In `UnstoreFile`:
```cpp
Array<byte> Buffer(File::CopyBufferSize());
while (1)
{
    uint Code=DataIO.UnpRead(&Buffer[0],Buffer.Size());
    ...
    Code=Code<DestUnpSize ? Code:(uint)DestUnpSize;
    DataIO.UnpWrite(&Buffer[0],Code);
```

The `Buffer` is allocated with `File::CopyBufferSize()`. `UnpRead` reads at most `Buffer.Size()` bytes. But then `Code` is set to `min(Code, DestUnpSize)`. If `DestUnpSize` is larger than `Buffer.Size()`, then `Code` could be larger than the buffer size, causing `UnpWrite` to read beyond the buffer.

Wait, but `Code` comes from `UnpRead` which returns at most `Count = Buffer.Size()`. So `Code <= Buffer.Size()`. Then `Code = min(Code, DestUnpSize)`. So `Code <= Buffer.Size()` still. Hmm.

Wait, let me re-read. `UnpRead` returns `ReadSize` which is `TotalRead`. In the loop, `Count` is decremented by `ReadSize`. But wait - there's a subtle bug. Look at the loop:

```cpp
while (Count > 0)
{
    ...
    ReadSize=SrcFile->Read(ReadAddr,SizeToRead);
    ...
    CurUnpRead+=ReadSize;
    TotalRead+=ReadSize;
    ...
    ReadAddr+=ReadSize;
    Count-=ReadSize;
    ...
    if (UnpVolume && UnpPackedSize == 0 && ...)
    {
        ...
        return -1;
    }
    else
      break;
}
```

The loop breaks after one iteration unless `UnpVolume` is set. So `TotalRead` can be at most `Count` (the buffer size). So `Code <= Buffer.Size()`.

